Usage: agent-deck config show --effective [path] [--json]
       agent-deck config get <key> [--json]
       agent-deck config set <key> <value> [--json]
       agent-deck config schema [--json]

Commands:
  get <key>                 Print one setting's effective value (its default when unset).
  set <key> <value>         Write one setting to config.toml with the TUI Settings writer;
                            prints the value read back. Lists are comma-separated.
  schema                    Every settable key: section, label, help, type, allowed values,
                            default, restart_required. The keys are the TUI Settings rows
                            plus recall.enabled, macapp.*, core.daemon (docs/macapp-core.md).
  show --effective [path]   Print merged [worktree] settings for path (default: cwd),
                            and which file supplied each value (default/global/dir-local).
                            Also known as `config explain` in the #2093 proposal.

Directory-local overrides (#2093): a .agent-deck/config.toml found in the target
directory or an ancestor (up to and including $HOME) can override default_location,
path_template, and sparse_checkout. Nearer files win; unknown keys are refused.

A dir-local file may come from a checkout you don't fully trust, so its
default_location/path_template values are bounded: an absolute path, a "~"-relative
path, or one that resolves outside the directory containing the outermost dir-local
config file for the target path is refused and falls back to the next source (an
outer dir-local file, then global config, then the built-in default). Global config
and --location/--template flags are unaffected and remain fully trusted. A refused
value and its fallback are shown by `config show --effective`.

Examples:
  agent-deck config show --effective
  agent-deck config show --effective ~/projects/example/feature-one
  agent-deck config show --effective . --json
